/E (2. Why Use SSIS?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
814 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (At the basic level, SSIS is used to extract data from common sources such as relational databases, flat files, and spreadsheets, apply simple data cleansing and transformation rules, and load the results into reporting or analytical databases. It supports automation of repetitive data tasks and provides built-in mechanisms for handling errors and basic logging.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
815 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (3. Core SSIS Architecture \(Basics\))
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
816 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (The foundational architecture of SSIS consists of the Control Flow, which defines the order in which tasks execute, and the Data Flow, which manages how data is extracted, transformed, and loaded. Connection Managers define how SSIS connects to source and destination systems, while variables and parameters allow packages to be flexible and reusable.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
817 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (4. Basic Data Flow Components)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
818 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (At an introductory level, learners work with common SSIS data flow components. These include sources such as SQL Server, Flat Files, Excel, and OLE DB, basic transformations like Derived Column and Conditional Split, and destinations such as SQL Server tables or flat files. These components enable simple yet effective data movement and transformation.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 329 0 R
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
